== Gemini's review of #711, 2026-11-06: three of four points real, fixed red first; one not a bug
1. greedy chain kvErr never reset: NOT A BUG. chainOpen starts a fresh chainLoop goroutine for every opening, and stopChain closes its request channel, which ends it, so kvErr lives for one opening; chainOpStart only ever arrives as an opening's first request. TestKVPages_chainReopensAfterAFailedTake, written red first, passes on the unfixed code: a look-ahead whose take is refused still answers the token in flight, and the next opening runs (and the test checks the take really was refused: slot 0 holds 1 block after it, 2 after the next opening). kvTakeFailForTest now covers the home block too, so the hook reaches every take.
2. a refused write keeps blocks: REAL, both halves. A write past 0 that the pool refuses partway kept the blocks it had taken, and a copy on write whose copy failed leaked its new block (counted, named by no table). Now a failure leaves the slot its sequence below the write and no block past it, and a failed copy gives its block back. Red first, TestKVPages_failedWriteHoldsNoBlocks: 312 blocks in use after the refused write against 307 before, slot 0 holding 161 against 156; a planted copy failure returned no error.
3. kvEvictIdle shields the bound slot: REAL. A batched step writes the slots it names, which the decoder marks busy (decoder: markSlot, MC3's holders), so a bound slot that has finished its turn is idle like any other; only MC3 generations mark slots, and without MC3 the one generation writes the bound slot, so the writer exclusion already covers it. Red first, TestKVPages_stepReleasesAnIdleBoundSlot: slot 1 released where slot 2 (bound, idle, written first) should have been. CUDA's kvEvictIdle has the same exclusion (backend/cuda/kvpool.go): not changed here.
4. blocks past a slot's sequence kept: REAL. A write ends the slot's sequence at its end (valid = hi), but only a write at 0 gave the blocks past it back, and a copy ending a longer destination gave none. Now every write gives them back first, where its takes can have them (kvTrim), a copy ends the destination's sequence the same way, and a copy declines a start past the destination's sequence (the trim would otherwise drop the copied blocks behind a nil error). Red first, TestKVPages_truncationReleasesTheTail: 10 blocks held after a write ended slot 0's 640 rows at 120; copies from a 120-row source of 640 rows, and from 64 into an empty slot, both accepted; 10 blocks held, 18 in the pool, after a 120-row copy ended slot 2's 640.
   This changes what KV2's G2 reads on Metal (TestKV2_copyKVExact, docs/measurements/kv2-2026-10-14.md): a copy that ends a longer destination now gives back the rows above the range (they read as block 0, zero) where they were kept; rows below the range, rows of a shorter destination and every other slot are unchanged as before. The decoder treats a destination as holding prompt[:hit] after a share and prefills from hit, and between the copy and that prefill other slots' steps can run and could have reclaimed from the stale tail.
   A write that gives blocks back stops the executor first (kvQuiesce, at every entry point on the caller's thread): a noHead forward's buffer can still be in flight after its ack, writing a block a rewind would free. ForwardBatch now maps after it stops the executor. TestKVPages_rewindQuiesces: after 70 pipelined positions an append keeps the executor, a decode at 10 and a 40-row prefill from 10 stop it.
   CopyKV's check that the source maps every block of the range now also covers a range past the source's sequence, since no slot maps a block past it.
G3d's step (3) refills the pool to mid-block before the step: it was full only because of the blocks point 2 leaked.
Plants, 10 of 10 red (08:21 to 08:24): 2a a refused write keeps its blocks (red in TestKVPages_failedWriteHoldsNoBlocks alone too, 08:23); 2b a failed copy keeps its block; 3 the bound slot shielded; 4a a write keeps the blocks past its end; 4b a copy keeps the destination's; 4c a copy from past the destination's sequence accepted; the quiesce off at the decode entry; off at the prefill pass; on for every write, an append too; the take hook missing the home block.
